From 271b34b798dfe4f1351bdf2f124c3981a8096b40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:46:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(motion): the rotary wrap turns its seam clear of a
 D-flat instead of refusing it

The wrap refused by name any work whose default seam (the basis' +-pi
branch) crossed a feature that is not turned, so a D-flat bar wrapped only
in some placements. Now, when the default seam is refused, the wrap
searches the azimuths midway between vertex columns for a seam where every
straddling facet stands on two alike meridian columns
(multiaxis::rotary_seam_angle), takes the middle of the widest turned
band, turns the azimuth basis there and lays the development out again
(multiaxis::rotary_sheet, the lap loop moved out of rotary_offset). The
turned sheet is re-judged by the same seam law, and the world exit maps
back through the same basis, so only the split moves - every point is cut
at its true azimuth. A seam that is already turned is kept, so every
existing wrap is byte-identical. A feature all the way round is still
refused by name.

Pinned by rotary_dflat::{an_off_centre_flat_across_the_seam_never_puts_a_tip_in_the_part
(all 36 placements wrap, 0 tips inside), a_d_flat_bar_wraps_in_every_placement
(channel tips at the flat's true azimuth),
a_flat_channel_across_the_default_seam_develops_under_adaptive_and_rings,
a_feature_all_the_way_round_is_still_refused_by_name}.

Fixes #226

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
